# Line endings
# ============================================================================
# Every text file is LF, in the working tree and in the repository.
#
# Nothing was pinning this before, so the endings a file had were whatever the
# last tool to touch it decided. On Windows that meant an edit could silently
# rewrite an entire file with CRLF while changing three lines of it — and the
# result is not a cosmetic problem. Twice during the UX pass a whole file
# showed as rewritten: a thirty-two line test addition appeared inside a
# 1010-line diff, and a fifty-line route change inside an 825-line one. A
# reviewer reading that diff cannot find the change, and neither can `git log
# -p` or `git blame` afterwards.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide per file whether something is text, so this needs
# no binary rules — a PNG added later is detected and left alone rather than
# being corrupted by a newline translation.
#
# `eol=lf` sets the working tree to LF as well as the repository. Without it a
# Windows checkout would still get CRLF, and the next edit would reintroduce
# exactly the problem this file exists to prevent.
#
# The repository was already entirely LF when this was added, so nothing is
# renormalized by it — it only stops a future edit from breaking that.
* text=auto eol=lf
